1. File Permissions (chmod)

Linux permissions =

r (read) = 4
w (write) = 2
x (execute) = 1
Example:
chmod 755 file.sh
Owner → 7 (rwx)
Group → 5 (r-x)
Others → 5 (r-x)
Explanation:

Linux uses a permission model based on read, write, and execute.

Numeric: 755
Symbolic: chmod u+x

Scripts need execute permission to run.

2. Hard vs Soft Links
Commands:
ln file.txt hardlink.txt
ln -s file.txt softlink.txt
Difference:
Type	Behavior
Hard Link	Shares same inode, survives original file deletion
Soft Link	Pointer to file, breaks if original is deleted
3. Process Management
🔹 ps aux – Snapshot Debugging

Scenario:
Production API is slow, but system is still responding

ps aux | grep java

Use Case:
Quick snapshot of running processes to identify abnormal resource usage

🔹 top / htop – Live Monitoring

Scenario:
Server CPU spikes to 80–100%

top
htop

Why:

Real-time CPU & memory usage
Sort:
Shift + P → CPU
Shift + M → Memory

Real Example:
During deployment, a container started looping → CPU spike → detected via htop

Interview Line:

“I use top/htop for real-time monitoring when there is a sudden spike in CPU or memory.”

🔹 kill -9 <pid> – Force Kill

Scenario:
Application is stuck / not responding

kill <pid>      # Graceful (SIGTERM)
kill -9 <pid>   # Force kill (SIGKILL)

Real Examples:

Jenkins job stuck
ECS task hung
Zombie processes

Interview Line:

“If a process doesn’t terminate gracefully, I use SIGKILL (-9) to forcefully stop it.”

🔹 Zombie Process (Important)

Check:

ps aux | grep Z

Why it happens:

Parent didn’t collect child exit status

Fix:

Restart parent process
Kill parent → system cleans zombie

Interview Line:

“Zombie processes occur when child processes exit but parent doesn’t read their status. I identify them using ps and resolve by restarting or killing the parent process.”

🔹 Foreground vs Background Jobs

Commands:

./deploy.sh &     # Run in background
jobs              # List jobs
fg %1             # Bring to foreground

Real Examples:

Long Terraform apply
Background log monitoring
🔥 Final Process Management Answer

“In real-time, I use ps aux for quick snapshots to identify processes, and top/htop for live monitoring during CPU or memory spikes. If a process is unresponsive, I first try graceful termination using kill, and if that fails, I use kill -9 to force stop it. I’ve also handled zombie processes by identifying them using ps and resolving by restarting or killing the parent process. Additionally, I use background and foreground job control when running long tasks like deployments.”

4. Disk & Memory Troubleshooting
🔹 df -h – Filesystem Usage

Scenario:
Disk alert → 90%+ full

df -h

Why:

Shows usage of /, /var, /home, etc.

Real Example:

/var full → logs → application crash

Interview Line:

“I use df -h to quickly check filesystem usage and identify which partition is running out of space.”

🔹 du -sh * – Directory Analysis

Scenario:
Need to find what is filling disk

cd /var
du -sh *

Deep Dive:

du -sh /var/log/*

Real Example:

Logs growing huge
Old backups not cleaned

Interview Line:

“After identifying a full filesystem using df, I use du to drill down and find which directories are consuming space.”

🔹 free -m – Memory Usage

Scenario:
Server slow / high memory alerts

free -m

Why:

Shows RAM usage
Shows swap usage

Key Insight:

Low memory + high swap = performance issue

Real Example:

Java app consuming high RAM → swapping → slow API

Interview Line:

“I use free -m to check RAM and swap usage. High swap usage usually indicates memory pressure affecting performance.”

🔥 Combined Real-Time Scenario (Very Important)
Scenario:

Website is slow or down

Step-by-Step Approach:
1. Check Disk:
df -h

➡️ If full → application may crash

2. Drill Down:
du -sh /var/*

➡️ Identify logs / backups

3. Check Memory:
free -m

➡️ Check RAM + swap

🔍 Correlation:
Issue	Root Cause
High Memory	Application issue
Disk Full	Logs / backups
🔥 Strong Final Interview Answer

“In real-time, I use df -h to check overall filesystem usage and identify if any partition is full. Once identified, I use du -sh to drill down into directories and find what is consuming space, such as logs or backups. For memory, I use free -m to monitor RAM and swap usage. High swap usage usually indicates memory pressure, which can impact application performance. I typically combine these checks during troubleshooting when a system becomes slow or unresponsive.”
